9front userland compatibility for 9vx.

Three changes, needed to run a 9front root file system under
"9vx -r":

* 9front creates files with the DMEXCL and DMTMP permission bits set
  (e.g. webcookies' jar lock); the Plan 9 4th edition kernel does not
  know DMTMP and devfs-posix rejects both bits in create, breaking
  such programs.  Accept the bits (exclusive use is not enforced on
  the host-backed file system anyway).

* Guard cclose(cnew) in the namec() create-error path.  When the
  create attempt fails, error() unwinds through gotolabel(), which
  restores the callee-saved registers to their values from before
  waserror() — including the one holding cnew, i.e. nil (GCC's own
  control-flow analysis proves this and even constant-folds the
  argument).  The unconditional cclose(nil) then panics the kernel
  with a segmentation fault.  Every failed create on a mount point
  hits this: 9front's rc creates /dev/wdir after each cd (for the rio
  window label), which fails on the #c mount without MCREATE, so any
  "cd" in a booted 9front crashed 9vx.

--- a/src/9vx/a/lib.h
+++ b/src/9vx/a/lib.h
@@ -72,6 +72,7 @@
 #define DMAPPEND	0x40000000	/* mode bit for append only files */
 #define DMEXCL		0x20000000	/* mode bit for exclusive use files */
 #define DMMOUNT		0x10000000	/* mode bit for mounted channel */
+#define DMTMP		0x04000000	/* mode bit for don't-archive file (9front) */
 #define DMREAD		0x4		/* mode bit for read permission */
 #define DMWRITE		0x2		/* mode bit for write permission */
 #define DMEXEC		0x1		/* mode bit for execute permission */
--- a/src/9vx/devfs-posix.c
+++ b/src/9vx/devfs-posix.c
@@ -413,7 +413,7 @@ fscreate(Chan *c, char *name, int mode, ulong perm)
 	if(mode & ~(OTRUNC|ORCLOSE|3))
 		error(Ebadarg);
 
-	if(perm & ~(DMDIR|0777))
+	if(perm & ~(DMDIR|DMEXCL|DMTMP|0777))
 		error(Ebadarg);
 
 	path0 = fspath(c, nil);
--- a/src/9vx/a/chan.c
+++ b/src/9vx/a/chan.c
@@ -1629,7 +1629,8 @@ namec(char *aname, int amode, ulong omode, ulong perm)
 			break;
 		}
 
-		/* create failed */
+		/* create failed */
+		if(cnew)
 		cclose(cnew);
 		if(m)
 			putmhead(m);
